cturers to authors of endoscopy guidelines in the United States. Gastrointest Endosc. 2020;91(2):266-273. doi: 10.1016/j.gie.2019.11.010 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 34. Combs T, Tritz D, Ivy H, von Borstel D, Horn J, Vassar M. Financial conflicts of interest among authors of clinical practice guidelines for routine screening mammography. J Am Coll Radiol. 2019;16(11):1598-1603. doi: 10.1016/j.jacr.2019.05.005 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 35. Eloy JA, Kilic S, Yoo NG, et al. Is industry funding associated with greater scholarly impact among academic neurosurgeons? World Neurosurg. 2017;103:517-525. doi: 10.1016/j.wneu.2017.03.110 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 36. Feng H, Wu P, Leger M. Exploring the industry-dermatologist financial relationship: insight from the Open Payment data. JAMA Dermatol. 2016;152(12):1307-1313. doi: 10.1001/jamadermatol.2016.3037 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 37. Ji YD, Peacock ZS. Shining sunlight on industry payments in oral and maxillofacial surgery: the Sunshine Act. J Oral Maxillofac Surg. 2019;77(4):685-689. doi: 10.1016/j.joms.2018.11.003 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 38. Sah S. Conflicts of interest and your physician: psychological processes that cause unexpected changes in behavior. J Law Med Ethics. 2012;40(3):482-487. doi: 10.1111/j.1748-720X.2012.00680.x [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 39. Association of American Medical Colleges. Physician education debt and the cost to medical school. Accessed April 1, 2022. https://store.aamc.org/downloadable/download/sample/sample_id/368/ 40. Grischkan J, George BP, Chaiyachati K, Friedman AB, Dorsey ER, Asch DA. Distribution of medical education debt by specialty, 2010-2016. JAMA Intern Med. 2017;177(10):1532-1535. doi: 10.1001/jamainternmed.2017.4023 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 41. Kane L. Physician compensation report 2021: the recovery begins. Medscape. Accessed April 1, 2022. https://www.medscape.com/slidesho