Ideno T, et al. Influence of contact with schizophrenia on implicit attitudes towards schizophrenia patients held by clinical residents. BMC Psychiatry. 2012;12:205. doi: 10.1186/1471-244X-12-205. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Peris TS, Teachman BA, Nosek BA. Implicit and explicit stigma of mental illness: links to clinical care. J Nerv Ment Dis. 2008;196(10):752–760. doi: 10.1097/NMD.0b013e3181879dfd. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Kroenke K. The interface between physical and psychological symptoms. Primary care companion. J Clin Psychiatry. 2003;5(suppl 7):11–18. [ Google Scholar ] 20. Greenwald AG, McGhee DE, Schwartz JL. Measuring individual differences in implicit cognition: the implicit association test. J Pers Soc Psychol. 1998;74(6):1464–1480. doi: 10.1037/0022-3514.74.6.1464. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Baker TK, Smith GS, Jacobs NN, et al. A deeper look at implicit weight bias in medical students. Adv in Health Sci Educ. 2017;22:889–900. doi: 10.1007/s10459-016-9718-1. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Greenwald AG, Nosek BA, Banaji MR. Understanding and using the implicit association test: I. An improved scoring algorithm. J Pers Soc Psychol. 2003;85(2):197–216. doi: 10.1037/0022-3514.85.2.197. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Teachman BA, Wilson JG, Komarovskaya I. Implicit and explicit stigma of mental illness in diagnosed and healthy samples. Journal of Social and Clinical Psychology. 2006;25(1):75–95. doi: 10.1521/jscp.2006.25.1.75. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Henderson C, Noblett J, Parke H, et al. Mental health-related stigma in health care and mental health-care settings. Lancet Psychiatry. 2014;1(6):467–482. doi: 10.1016/S2215-0366(14)00023-6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Schulze B. Stigma and mental health professionals: a review of the evidence on an intricate relationship. Int Rev Psychiatry. 2007;19(2):137–155. doi: 10.1080/09540260701278929. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Maron BA, Fein S, Maron BJ, Hillel AT, El Baghdadi MM, Rodenhauser P. Ability of prospective assessment of personality profiles to predict the practice specialty of medical students. Proc (Bayl Univ Med Cent). 2007;20(1):22–26. doi: 10.1080/08998280.2007.11928228. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Sievert M, Zwir I, Cloninger KM, Lester N, Rozsa S, Cloninger CR. The influence of temperament and character profiles on specialty choice and well-being in medical residents. PeerJ. 2016;4:e2319. doi: 10.7717/peerj.2319. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Chapman E, Kaatz A, Carnes M. Physicians and implicit bias: how doctors may unwittingly perpetuate health care disparities. JGIM. 2013;28(11):1504–10. doi: 10.1007/s11606-013-2441-1. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Ostrow L, Manderscheid R, Mojtabai R. Stigma and difficulty accessing medical care in a sample of adults with serious mental illness. J Health Care Poor Underserved. 2014;25(4):1956–1965. doi: 10.1353/hpu.2014.0185. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. Keller AO, Valdez CR, Schwei RJ, Jacobs EA. Disclosure of depression in primary care: a qualitative study of women’s perceptions. Womens Health Issues. 2016;26(5):529–536. doi: 10.1016/j.whi.2016.07.002. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',286,'2025-10-13 12:56:11','2025-10-13 12:56:11');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2310,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12515465','Defining the scope of point-of-care ultrasound in internal medicine: a consensus statement by the point-of-care special interest group of the Internal Medicine Society of Australia and New Zealand.','Cid-Serra X, Gibbs H, Szecket N, Comeau D, Elliott P, Duong V, Perera P, Thorson P, O''Donovan R, Maguire G, Hean RV, Potter E.','2025','equips imsanz panellists imsanz panellists unduly reintroduced disapprove arthrocentesis gravid racp excursion arthrocentesis hypovolaemic postrenal expediting collapsibility arthrocentesis teleultrasound recognises racp imj imj imj gallard redonnet bourcier deshaies largeteau amalric terrazas solis garay juarez kobal trento baharami tolstrup naqvi cercek donroe marbach almufleh simard trappey schnobrich razi royse canty e2138228 ansary canty royse royse royse canty royse lasserson laursen sloth lambrechtsen mantuani frazee fahimi nagdev macho aro bruckner cogliati gilja gurghean iwy arishenkoff desy ailon cimus e32829 schnobrich dancel macho garmilla ezquerra canora lebrato rojo iwy iwy steinmetz weerdenburg olszynski heslop bhagra sekiguchi johannessen uthaug reite ovrebotten einvik myhre chron pulmon meziere meziere volpicelli cardinale garofalo veltri lammoza peris tutino zagli batacchi cianchi zdnv marill sajed bertuzzi liteplo maximizes lindelius torngren pettersson adami laursen sloth davidsen lambrechtsen feiley eadie telesonography e0194840 tupchong verceles mccurdy teleultrasound safadi mccurdy imj imj imj','Abstract Background Point‐of‐care ultrasound (POCUS) complements physical examination to advance diagnosis and treatment in a range of clinical scenarios. However, the scope for adult internal medicine physicians is not defined in Australia and New Zealand. Aim To outline the clinical scope and core competencies of POCUS for internal medicine physicians in Australia and New Zealand using a consensus process. Methods A steering committee and expert panel utilised the Delphi method, comprising rounds of questionnaires and anonymous feedback, to define scope by determining (i) clinical scenarios in which POCUS provides benefit and (ii) core competencies in scanning. An 80% agreement cut‐off was used in each round for both inclusion and exclusion of items, and items could be added depending on feedback. Synthesis of qualitative feedback was included. Results The expert panel endorsed the use of POCUS in eight clinical scenarios: assessment of fluid status, undifferentiated shock or hypotension, dyspnoea/respiratory failure, acute kidney injury, suspected venous thromboembolism, synovitis/joint effusion and for guiding invasive procedures and diuretic therapy in heart failure. Core competencies based on identification of organ system pathology were endorsed, incorporating abnormalities of the heart, lung, inferior vena cava, abdomen, peripheral veins, soft tissue collection and joint effusion. The consensus intended not to mandate POCUS use nor limit the scope for physicians with advanced training, but offer a structured schema. Conclusion We successfully established a consensus on the POCUS scope for internal medicine in our region. This provides a foundation to develop formal training and credentialing. Keywords: consensus statement, internal medicine, ultrasound Introduction Point‐of‐care ultrasound (POCUS) equips clinicians with diagnostic information that advances patient care more rapidly and accurately than conventional physical examination alone. 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 , 7 It differs from ultrasound performed in the cardiology or radiology departments in its purpose, scope and user. Unlike comprehensive, protocol‐driven ultrasound studies, POCUS is a bedside tool used by the treating clinician to answer specific clinical questions in real time, facilitating immediate decision‐making. POCUS is particularly suited to the practice of internal medicine, a speciality with expertise in undifferentiated presentations and complex comorbidity. POCUS prompts modification of primary diagnoses in up to 25% of patients who present acutely to internal medicine services 8 and leads to management adjustment in over half of all patients. 9 , 10 Its application extends beyond the acute hospital inpatient setting and into follow‐up clinics and the facilitation of home‐based acute care. 11 Adoption of POCUS in Australia and New Zealand is largely confined to certain specialties such as emergency and intensive care medicine. Indeed, it is considered a requirement for completion of intensive care training. However, adoption by internal medicine is in its infancy, lacking both a defined pathway to competency and inclusion in the training curriculum. This contrasts with countries such as Canada and the United Kingdom, which have structured training programmes, supported by professional bodies and approved departmental supervisors. Individual specialties must determine how POCUS is applicable to their practice, including the type of images captured and variables measured. In internal medicine, where clinicians manage a wide range of presentations, a multi‐organ approach to POCUS is essential. 12 Indeed, as an example, multi‐organ scanning protocols in dyspnoea demonstrate significantly improved diagnostic accuracy and may even reduce length of hospital stay. 8 , 9 , 13 , 14 Developing a standard set of indications or scenarios where POCUS provides benefit in adult internal medicine, along with the competencies expected for its use, may be considered the foundation upon which training, and ultimately widespread adoption, is built. While such standards have been defined internationally, 15 , 16 it is crucial to develop a local consensus that reflects specific patient needs, clinical context, credentialing and governance requirements and healthcare system structures. Accordingly, we recently published the rationale for local consensus. 12 This consensus statement from the Internal Medicine Society of Australia and New Zealand (IMSANZ) POCUS special interest group outlines the scope for POCUS in general internal medicine specialists in Australia and New Zealand. The scope pertains to (i) the clinical scenarios in which POCUS may be used and (ii) core competencies in scanning. Methods Study design This is a consensus study using the Delphi method. 17 The study included a steering committee and a panel of experts. The process involved iterative rounds with no predefined limit of rounds, predefined criteria for determining agreement, anonymous responses and controlled feedback to the experts after each round. Steering committee A small steering committee, composed of two members (X. Cid‐Serra and E. Potter), oversaw the study. The committee was responsible for performing an initial literature review to identify relevant information and existing competency frameworks, creating the first survey and refining the survey content between rounds. They also reviewed and incorporated feedback from expert participants throughout the process and ensured methodological rigour and consistency across all stages of the study. Expert panel We assembled an expert panel of 21 members. The panellists were physicians practising in internal medicine who met one of the following criteria: (i) possession of POCUS certification or (ii) a minimum of 10 years of POCUS experience without certification. Experts were identified through dissemination of an expression of interest to members of the IMSANZ, including advanced trainees, in addition to personal invitations extended by the steering committee to suitable individuals. Questionnaire structure The steering committee developed a user‐friendly version with clear, question‐based prompts to collect focused feedback on each item. Most questions'' structure followed a Likert scale, ranging from ‘strongly disagree’ to ‘strongly agree’. The initial questionnaire (Appendix S1 ) included (i) clinical scenarios in which physicians should be proficient in using POCUS, (ii) organs that physicians should be able to scan, (iii) imaging variables that should be assessed for each organ and (iv) ultrasound‐guided invasive procedures. Data collection and anonymity Once responses were submitted, the steering committee conducted two levels of analysis. First, a quantitative analysis to identify items with agreement, and second, a qualitative analysis was conducted to review and organise all comments. All survey questions were administered using an online platform (Qualtrics XM). Panellists provided demographic information without personal identifiers. This ensured that the steering committee was blinded to the identities of the participants during data analysis and when reviewing comments and responses. This anonymity was maintained to prevent bias and ensure that no single expert''s views unduly influenced others. Consensus process and rounds For each questionnaire item we predefined an 80% agreement threshold, that is items with 80% agreement (responses of ‘agree’ or ‘strongly agree’) were included in the final draft. Conversely, items with 80% disagreement (responses of ‘disagree’ or ‘strongly disagree’) were excluded from subsequent rounds. Items not meeting these thresholds were revised based on qualitative feedback and reintroduced in the next round for further discussion. The Likert scale used in the study included the option ‘maybe’, allowing participants to express uncertainty or conditional agreement. For responses marked as ‘maybe’, a comment section was provided for participants to elaborate on their reasoning. The steering committee carefully reviewed the explanations accompanying ‘maybe’ responses. If the explanation suggested that the skill could be performed as an advanced or optional competency, this response was interpreted as aligning with ‘disagree’ or ‘strongly disagree’. This approach ensured that the consensus focused exclusively on a minimum skill set. Qualitative feedback from participant comments was summarised and presented to the expert panel in subsequent rounds. This allowed participants to review and consider the collective input before re‐evaluating the item. Suggestions for additional items were incorporated into the next round of the survey to be discussed. Final approval Once consensus was reached, a final draft of the document was distributed to the expert panel for approval. Participants voted to either approve or disapprove of the final draft, completing the consensus process. Results The expert panel was composed of 21 physicians. Most respondents identified as male (76%) and 24% as female. Geographically, 86% of respondents were based in Australia and 14% in New Zealand. The majority of Australian physicians were based in Victoria (68%), followed by Queensland (16%), New South Wales (5%) and Western Australia (5%). In terms of seniority, the majority (90%) were consultants, with 62% having over 10 years of experience in internal medicine. A total of 76% reported having some qualification in POCUS and 95% as having clinical experience using POCUS. In terms of specialty discipline, 52% of respondents were specialists in general and acute care medicine, 10% were respiratory physicians, 5% were cardiologists, 5% were emergency medicine, 19% had dual training including general and acute care medicine, and 9% were general medicine trainees. Consensus was reached after three Delphi rounds (Figure 1 ). In the first two rounds, experts defined which clinical scenarios and core competencies should be included or excluded, based on ≥80% agreement. The final round served to endorse the completed scope and competency document. Qualitative feedback was incorporated throughout. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Flowchart of Delphi consensus process. This flowchart outlines the three‐round Delphi process. In the first two rounds, there were items endorsed and items excluded by the experts. The third round focused on the final endorsement of the consolidated proposal. At each stage, the steering committee collected experts'' responses, incorporated revisions and circulated anonymised feedback to the panel for further reflection and consensus building. Round 1 The steering committee received 21 responses from the experts. The results of the first round are summarised in Figure 2 . While average scores are presented in the figure to provide a clear visual representation of the data, they were not used to define consensus. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Average agreement scores across clinical indications and anatomical targets. This bar chart shows the average scores from the Delphi expert survey evaluating agreement on the minimal POCUS skills for general medicine physicians. Responses were rated on a 5‐point Likert scale (1 = strongly disagree to 5 = strongly agree). Towards the left (low score) indicates strong disagreement and suggests items to be excluded, while towards the right (high score) indicates strong agreement and supports inclusion. The orange line represents an approximate threshold for agreement, included for visual reference. From the 62 items assessed, 24 met the inclusion threshold – defined as ≥80% of respondents selecting ‘agree’ or ‘strongly agree’ – and were considered recommended competencies for general medicine physicians. These included the use of POCUS for ultrasound‐guided procedures, fluid status assessment, evaluation of dyspnoea, hypotension or shock, and guiding diuretic therapy in patients with heart failure. Anatomical targets included the lungs, heart and inferior vena cava. Within specific diagnostic domains, competencies such as the identification of pleural effusion, pericardial effusion, free intraperitoneal fluid, urinary retention and basic cardiac chamber size and function also reached consensus for inclusion. Regarding ultrasound‐guided procedures, the majority met the criteria for inclusion as core competencies. However, pericardiocentesis and arthrocentesis did not meet the thresholds for either inclusion or exclusion and were therefore carried forward for further evaluation in the subsequent Delphi round. Items meeting the exclusion threshold – defined as ≥80% of respondents selecting ‘disagree’ or ‘strongly disagree’ – included the use of intestinal and thyroid ultrasound; and for abdominal applications, the assessment of small bowel obstruction and inflammatory bowel disease. In the cardiac domain, excluded competencies included the assessment of left ventricular diastolic function using spectral Doppler, evaluation of valvular disease severity with spectral Doppler and assessment of right ventricular systolic function using spectral Doppler. Three new items were proposed by the experts for the subsequent Delphi round: ultrasound‐guided lumbar puncture, assessment of the gravid uterus and ultrasound assessment of peripheral nerves. In the comment sections, key themes included the importance of its integration in remote or resource‐limited settings. Concerns were raised about maintaining skills in less commonly performed procedures, such as pericardiocentesis, and ensuring competency through sufficient procedural experience, highlighting the importance of adhering to established guidelines and specialist training college‐recommended competency frameworks. Round 2 The steering committee provided a summary table of the results of the first round (Appendix S2 ) and created a second questionnaire including the feedback and comments received (Appendix S3 ). Based on comments from the first round, the committee added additional information about ultrasound‐guided procedures to support decision‐making: whether the procedure is expected for physicians according to the current Royal Australasian College of Physicians (RACP) training curriculum and whether international guidelines recommend performing the procedure under ultrasound guidance. Additionally, three new items were included based on suggestions from the first‐round comments. In the second round, the steering committee received responses from 18 out of the 21 experts. As responses were submitted anonymously, several reminders were sent to all participants, but the identities of the three non‐responders could not be determined. In the second round, the following items met the criteria for inclusion: basic assessment of significant cardiac valve pathology, identification of joint effusion and guidance for knee aspiration, and ultrasound‐guided lumbar puncture. Three cardiac POCUS items did not meet the thresholds for inclusion or exclusion due to a significant proportion of ‘maybe’ responses. Following qualitative review of the comments by the steering committee, the uncertainty was attributed to experts classifying this skill as advanced and, therefore, excluded. These items were measurement of cardiac chamber diameters and areas, assessment of right ventricular function using Tricuspid Annular Plane Systolic Excursion and estimation of left ventricular ejection fraction using the Simpson method. All remaining items in the second round met the criteria for exclusion, including ultrasound guided pericardiocentesis and shoulder arthrocentesis. Round 3: final consensus After the second round, no items required further discussion. A final version of the consensus was subsequently drafted, incorporating all items that met the inclusion criteria in either round 1 or round 2. These competencies were organised according to the same framework used throughout the survey: clinical scenarios, organs to scan, key variables to assess for each organ and ultrasound‐guided procedures. The final version was distributed to the expert panel and received full endorsement from all members. Consensus on clinical scenario‐based POCUS applications The panel endorsed incorporating POCUS in eight clinical scenarios where it is expected to enhance diagnostic accuracy, improve procedural safety and effectively guide therapeutic interventions (Table 1 ). Table 1. Summary of consensus clinical scenarios for POCUS integration and respective benefits Clinical scenario Benefits of adding POCUS Invasive procedures Enhances procedure safety. Decrease number of attempts and rate of complications. Fluid status assessment Provides non‐invasive, real‐time insights into volume status, aiding decisions regarding fluid resuscitation or restriction. Diagnostic assessment of undifferentiated hypotension or shock Rapid differentiation of shock aetiologies, including hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, obstructive (e.g. tamponade, pulmonary embolism) or distributive (e.g. septic) Diagnostic assessment of dyspnoea, hypoxia and respiratory failure Provides valuable information to differentiate respiratory from cardiac causes. Guiding diuretic therapy in heart failure Evaluates degree of pulmonary congestion and fluid overload supporting the optimisation of diuretic therapy in patients with heart failure. Diagnostic assessment of acute kidney injury Assessment of postrenal cause by identifying hydronephrosis. This approach facilitates timely intervention. Diagnostic assessment of deep venous thrombosis and pulmonary embolism Can directly diagnose DVT and provide indirect evidence of PE, such as dilated right ventricle or D‐shape interventricular septum, expediting clinical decision‐making. Diagnostic assessment of synovitis and joint effusion Visualisation of synovitis and joint effusions, supporting diagnostic accuracy and guiding therapeutic procedures such as aspiration or injection Open in a new tab Consensus on core competencies in scanning: organs and imaging variables The panel endorsed POCUS scanning of the following organs and body structures: lungs, heart, inferior vena cava, peritoneal space (ascites), kidneys, bladder, central and peripheral veins and arteries, joints and soft tissue. The specific variables that should be assessed for each organ or structure are outlined in Table 2 . Table 2. Ultrasound parameters by organ system Organ Variables to assess Heart Presence of pericardial effusion Visual assessment of ventricular systolic function Visual assessment of cardiac chambers'' size Estimation of the size of pericardial effusion Assessment of echocardiographic signs of tamponade based solely on 2D images Visual assessment of the valves for significant stenosis or regurgitation based on 2D images and colour Doppler Lung Pleural effusion B lines/interstitial syndrome Collapse/consolidation Pneumothorax Inferior vena cava (IVC) IVC diameter IVC collapsibility Abdomen Free fluid/ascites Presence of hydronephrosis (kidneys) and bladder (urinary retention) Abdominal aortic aneurysm Other Identify subcutaneous collection Identify carotid artery versus jugular vein Identify venous thrombosis Identify joint effusion Open in a new tab Consensus on ultrasound‐guided procedures The panel recommended that clinicians performing the following procedures in their clinical practice should preferably learn and utilise ultrasound guidance to enhance safety, precision, procedural success rate and minimise complications. Paracentesis Thoracentesis Central line catheter placement Knee arthrocentesis Arterial line placement Lumbar puncture Peripheral vein cannulation Discussion Using the Delphi method, we successfully established consensus on the scope of POCUS use in internal medicine for Australia and New Zealand. We established that general physicians performing POCUS should be competent in the assessment of patients presenting with certain clinical situations through targeted imaging of several organ systems. The clinical scenarios include patients presenting with dyspnoea, respiratory failure, hypotension or shock, acute kidney injury, heart failure, venous thromboembolism, acute arthritis and for the performance of invasive procedures. The organ systems and anatomical areas included the heart, lungs, great vessels, abdomen and joints. The findings of our consensus are similar to other consensus processes and position statements from other internal medicine societies around the world, 18 , 19 , 20 , 21 reflecting a shared understanding among physicians of how POCUS should be incorporated into clinical practice. The clinical scenarios described are encountered daily in internal medicine, a specialty expert in undifferentiated presentations in patients with multiple comorbidities. 22 , 23 For example, patients with a history of both chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and heart failure may present with dyspnoea, and often there is doubt following the initial clinical assessment as to the predominant acute pathology. The demonstration of pulmonary congestion by POCUS at the bedside helps to define the likely diagnosis and guide initial therapy. 24 , 25 , 26 , 27 Diagnostic certainty may be further enhanced by an assessment of left ventricular function and of cardiac valvular function. In addition, the detection of life‐threatening conditions, such as cardiac tamponade, facilitates urgent therapy without the delay of more formal imaging. 28 Notably, several items were excluded not because they were viewed as unimportant, but because they were considered to represent more advanced skills or beyond the scope of general medicine. This approach ensures that the consensus does not limit the scope of practice but rather delineates a clear, achievable baseline for training and performance expectations. POCUS offers significant benefits to clinical workflow and broader health system efficiency. While it does not replace comprehensive diagnostic imaging, it can provide timely, actionable information that enables early initiation of appropriate treatment. This can help reduce delays associated with formal imaging pathways and allow clinicians to reassess the urgency or necessity of additional investigations based on bedside findings. Previous studies showed that POCUS use is associated with reduced utilisation of chest X‐rays, chest computed tomography and unnecessary echocardiograms. 29 At the same time, when significant findings are identified – such as severe aortic stenosis – it prompts appropriate referral for comprehensive imaging. 10 This dual function supports more efficient and targeted use of diagnostic investigations. Its portability and immediacy make POCUS particularly valuable in resource‐limited settings, remote areas and even in home or residential aged care environments. For example, the use of lung ultrasound to confirm pneumonia in a patient at a residential aged care facility may allow for prompt antibiotic treatment without the need for an emergency department visit for chest radiography. In such contexts, POCUS can help avoid unnecessary hospital transfers, reduce healthcare burdens and improve patient comfort and outcomes. POCUS use has also been shown to positively impact patient satisfaction with their medical care and understanding of their medical condition. 30 , 31 , 32 Finally, there is evidence suggesting that novice‐performed ultrasound with real‐time guidance from a remotely located expert (teleultrasound) can be feasible and accurate in diagnosing pathology in many clinical contexts and body systems, further addressing barriers to accessing specialist care. 33 , 34 , 35 While several clinical scenarios and core scanning competencies were designated as within scope for internal medicine, this consensus does not mandate the use of POCUS or expect all core competencies to be achieved for its application. The need for general physicians to perform invasive procedures may differ between metropolitan and rural settings. The group recognises that experienced physicians may continue performing procedures without POCUS where it is unavailable. Conversely, this statement does not preclude general physicians from developing greater expertise in certain areas of diagnostic ultrasound, such as advanced echocardiography. Implications for adoption of POCUS in internal medicine While internal medicine physicians in Australia and New Zealand are already performing POCUS, it is in a low volume and with different competencies and varied training. While this consensus statement represents an important advancement, it marks only the first stage in the broader implementation of POCUS in internal medicine. The next steps should involve the development of a detailed curriculum and a structured training pathway aligned with the defined competencies. This should take into account the diagnostic accuracy of each type of ultrasound examination and its role in clinical decision‐making – for example, ruling in or ruling out pathology, guiding fluid management or identifying the need for further investigations. This must then be linked at the health care organisation level to credentialing and the scope of practice for general physicians. Other considerations include maintenance of competence and quality assurance activities that should then be part of departmental quality and safety activities. In the longer term, engaging relevant sonographic groups and collaboration with the RACP is essential to consider incorporating POCUS into general and acute care medicine advanced training, ensuring it becomes an integrated and sustained component of physician education. Strengths and limitations Our study employed a rigorous methodology, maintaining experts'' anonymity and incorporating feedback between rounds. The final draft was endorsed by all panel members, ensuring strong consensus on the final set of competencies. An additional strength of our process was the diverse representation of experts from various regions across Australia and New Zealand, which promoted the inclusion of broad experience and perspectives. However, our study carries the inherent limitations of the Delphi method, including reliance on expert opinion, which introduces subjectivity. Another limitation is the underrepresentation of rural and remote areas when those settings potentially stand to benefit most from POCUS due to limited access to traditional imaging services. Conclusion Using the Delphi method, we have defined a consensus on the range of clinical scenarios and scanning competencies relevant to POCUS use in internal medicine in the Australian and New Zealand context. This provides a foundation upon which to develop formal training and credentialing frameworks which are necessary to translate this consensus statement into widespread adoption, ultimately enhancing clinical decision‐making and improving patient care in internal medicine. Supporting information Appendix S1. Supporting Information. IMJ-55-1677-s002.pdf (1.1MB, pdf) Appendix S2. Supporting Information. IMJ-55-1677-s001.pdf (81.9KB, pdf) Appendix S3. Supporting Information. IMJ-55-1677-s003.pdf (61.9KB, pdf) Acknowledgements Open access publishing facilitated by The University of Melbourne, as part of the Wiley ‐ The University of Melbourne agreement via the Council of Australian University Librarians. Funding: None. Conflict of interest: None. References 1. Gallard E, Redonnet JP, Bourcier JE, Deshaies D, Largeteau N, Amalric JM et al . Diagnostic performance of cardiopulmonary ultrasound performed by the emergency physician in the management of acute dyspnea. Am J Emerg Med 2015; 33: 352–358. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Keil‐Rios D, Terrazas‐Solis H, Gonzalez‐Garay A, Sanchez‐Avila JF, Garcia‐Juarez I. Pocket ultrasound device as a complement to physical examination for ascites evaluation and guided paracentesis. Intern Emerg Med 2016; 11: 461–466. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Kobal SL, Trento L, Baharami S, Tolstrup K, Naqvi TZ, Cercek B et al . Comparison of effectiveness of hand‐carried ultrasound to bedside cardiovascular physical examination. Am J Cardiol 2005; 96: 1002–1006. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Liu RB, Donroe JH, McNamara RL, Forman HP, Moore CL. The practice and implications of finding fluid during point‐of‐care ultrasonography: a review. JAMA Intern Med 2017; 177: 1818–1825. [ DO