INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2322,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11918057','Concerns and recommendations of internal medicine residents in HIV patient care in the second decade of the AIDS epidemic in Taiwan.','Hsiung PC, Tsai YF.','2004','sande currey gerbert badner dworkin cooksey gerbert bleecker corbin newbury mccaan sharkey valimaki suominet peate eason baccalaureate gignac oermann','Abstract Internal medicine residents are usually on the front line of caring for HIV‐infected patients. In the second decade of the AIDS epidemic in Taiwan, a qualitative study was conducted to recognize the concerns of internal medicine residents in caring for HIV/AIDS patients and to identify possible strategies to promote HIV patient care that have not been addressed in previous research. Individual in‐depth interviews were conducted with 47 internal medicine residents at a university hospital in Taiwan. Seven factors (stereotypes of AIDS; fear of transmission; concerns of families, friends, and colleagues; lack of clinical expertise; lack of collegial support; lack of a sense of accomplishment; and lack of worker''s compensation if infected) were found to be the chief concerns of participants in providing HIV/AIDS care. Four strategies (education, clinical experience, safe working environment, and employee benefits) were voiced as those that could promote health care professionals'' well‐being and willingness to provide HIV/AIDS care. The results of this study are explained in light of the historical, social, and medical contexts in Taiwan. Keywords: HIV care, internal medicine resident Full Text The Full Text of this article is available as a PDF (192.9 KB). References 1. Cooke M, Sande MA. The HIV epidemic and training in internal medicine: challenge and recommendations. N Engl J Med. 1989; 321: 1334–1337. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Cooke M. Supporting health care workers in the treatment of HIV‐infected patients. Prim Care. 1992; 19: 245–246. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Currey CJ, Johnson M, Ogden B. Willingness of health‐ professions students to treat patients with AIDS. Acad Med. 1990; 65: 472–474. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Gerbert B, Maguire B, Badner V, et al. Why fear persists: health care professionals and AIDS. JAMA. 1988; 260: 3481–3483. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Dworkin J, Albrecht G, Cooksey J. Concern about AIDS among hospital physicians, nurses and social workers. Soc Sci Med. 1991; 33: 239–248. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Robinson N. People with HIV/AIDS: who cares?. J Adv Nurs. 1998; 28: 771–778. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Gerbert B, Maguire BT, Bleecker T, et al. Primary care physicians and AIDS: attitudinal and structural barriers to care. JAMA. 1991; 266: 2837–2842. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Strauss A, Corbin J. Basics of Qualitative Research: Grounded Theory Procedures and Techniques. Newbury Park, CA: Sage. 1990. [ Google Scholar ] 9. Wachter RM, Cooke M, Hopewell PC, et al. Attitudes of medical residents regarding intensive care for patients with the acquired immunodeficiency syndrome. Arch Intern Med. 1988; 148: 149–152. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. McCaan TV, Sharkey RJ. Educational intervention with international nurses and changes in knowledge, attitudes and willingness to provide care to patients with HIV/AIDS. J Adv Nurs. 1998; 27: 267–273. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Valimaki M, Suominet T, Peate I. Attitudes of professionals, students and the general public to HIV/AIDS and people with HIV/AIDS: a review of the research. J Adv Nurs. 1998; 27: 752–759. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Tierney AJ. HIV/AIDS – knowledge, attitudes and education of nurses: a review of the research. J Clin Nurs. 1995; 4: 13–21. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Bowman JM, Brown ST, Eason FR. Attitudes of baccalaureate nursing students in one school toward acquired immune deficiency syndrome. AIDS Educ Prev. 1994; 6: 535–541. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Gignac D, Oermann MH. Willingness of nursing students and faculty to care for patients with AIDS. Am J Infect Control. 1991; 19: 191–197. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',8,'2025-10-13 12:59:20','2025-10-13 12:59:20');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2323,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11468379','The "flipped visit": an innovative method to improve medical student self-efficacy through a structured approach in clinic.','Langer AL, Parnes AD, Kumar NL, Kesselheim JC, Osman NY.','2024','doctoring postulates expend begets enactive educationally dueling dissatisfying hematologists engendering subspecialities hematologists posited entrust preassigned preassigned philosophically dispersing nonevaluative gammopathy passiveeducational subspecialities reapply levers harmoniously unentrustable checkbox hematologists interceding accustomed levers countervailing purposefulness expend nlk jck nyo flexner carnegie artino lightsey levinsohn kayoumi madero hoots abkowitz coller dimichele okpechi tummalapalli chothia tungsanga caskey supplement2 ii43 faig bodio downey shpilsky leahy wischhusen hematologists e927 parzonka ndayishimiye domagała flipping treglia inverting spivak ghanima ghanima gernsheimer kuter itp kearon barcellini fattizzo artino dezee gilliland waechter cruess riverhead sturgis levers competencyworks inacol ed590519 thoonen scherpbier fluit crmg stalmeijer charokar footstep learne konings touchie rmh rafiq colonnello farinelli samora clohisy e76 ishak nikravesh lederer ogunyemi','Abstract Background Specialty clinics can be challenging for students on the internal medicine clerkship. They often lack the specialized knowledge necessary to fully engage in the clinic and may be pushed into an observational role, rather than being afforded meaningful opportunities. These peripheral roles undermine self-efficacy, and, therefore, education and interest. We sought to improve self-efficacy of students attending non-malignant hematology clinic, as well as to enhance interest in hematology and internal medicine. Methods We developed a flipped visit model analogous to the flipped classroom. Students each received pre-readings accompanied and pre-assigned cases with delineated reasons for referral and learning objectives. This model allowed students to prepare the hematology content for visits prior to arrival and then focus their time in clinic on executing visits. Participating students completed pre- and post-clinic surveys. These surveys covered core concepts in self-efficacy and interest in hematology and internal medicine on a five-point Likert scale. Results Of 103 students attending hematology clinic, 38 students (37%) opted to participate. Two of the 38 did not complete the post-clinic survey. Students had a statistically significant increase in four of six measures of self-efficacy: evaluation of research relevant to patient care and practicing cost-effective care ( p = 0.008 and 0.001, respectively); and creation of a differential diagnosis and treatment plan specific to hematology (both p < 0.001). While pre-clinic student responses expressed interest in more exposure to hematology or a possible career in hematology or internal medicine, this was not changed post-clinic. When comparing their experience to other clinics, 75% and 71% students rated hematology clinic slightly or much better than other medicine clinics and non-medicine clinics, respectively. The flipped visit improved self-efficacy both specific to hematology and more generally. While students rated the experience more highly than other clinics, there was no impact on career interest in hematology or internal medicine. Conclusion A flipped visit approach to incorporating medical students into hematology clinic that included pre-assigned cases and readings improved self-efficacy and was preferred by students. They may be adopted easily in other ambulatory education settings. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12909-024-06122-6. Keywords: Undergraduate medical education, Ambulatory education, Flipped classroom, Self-efficacy Background In the American educational model, medical students are faced with the challenge of mastering the cognitive, affective and psychomotor skills of doctoring during short, sequential block rotations [ 1 , 2 ]. Although ambulatory practice comprises the majority of primary and specialty care, the current US educational model continues to limit student engagement in outpatient practice. This creates particularly daunting challenges in specialty ambulatory clinics such as non-malignant hematology, where students may be overwhelmed by the pressure to acquire knowledge about hematologic disorders simultaneous with learning the skills needed to conduct a clinic visit. From historical discussions with core clerkship students, we also understand that students most enjoy the clinics in which they engage in direct patient care, which is consistent with self-efficacy theory [ 3 ]. Self-efficacy theory focuses on the impact that self-perceived capability has on achieving educational outcomes. It postulates that those who perceive themselves as more capable will persist when they encounter challenges and exert more effort to advance their education or “expend more effort and persist longer,” while individuals with low self-efficacy will limit their growth [ 4 ]. Thus, perceived efficacy begets both greater perceived efficacy and increased experience and objective efficacy in a positive feedback loop. Enactive mastery (e.g. conducting a visit) is the most effective method of improving self-efficacy, while more passive roles are less effective [ 3 – 5 ]. Exposure to ambulatory care and fields that are heavily weighted towards outpatient care are educationally vital. However, it can be difficult for students to retain an active rather than observational role given the need to simultaneously acquire the knowledge, skills, and attitude to conduct a successful ambulatory encounter. Additionally,, the appointment length and rigid schedule of clinic create a difficult situation where faculty must balance the dueling priorities of patient care and teaching. These considerations can push students into an observational role that undermines self-efficacy and educational growth. Equally problematic, a passive and dissatisfying experience may limit students’ interest in pursuing a career in non-malignant hematology or internal medicine. Given the projected deficit of non-malignant hematologists and difficulty recruiting students to this subspecialty [ 6 , 7 ], this represents a serious long-term consequence for the health-care system. While we note this shortage in the context of hematologist in the United States, this has been established in general medicine and other medicine subspecialties across the globe [ 8 – 11 ]. As such, engendering interest is relevant in other training systems and subspecialities. There is a lack of established strategies known to improve the education experience for medical students participating in ambulatory hematology care, despite evidence that a large proportion of those who elect to become benign hematologists develop their interest during medical school rather than in latter stages of training [ 6 ]. With these consideration in mind, we sought to improve self-efficacy in the setting of non-malignant hematology clinic in order to better serve medical student education and engender a long term interest in a career in hematology and internal medicine. Appreciating that the fund of knowledge needed for hematology clinic is expected to be in evolution for students and often exceeds that expected for an experienced general medicine physician, we posited that a key feature of a successful intervention would be to augment their knowledge specific to the reason for referral. Without this preparation, it had been difficult to entrust students to collect all relevant history and laboratory data to discuss a case. We term the combination of the ability to review charts in advance, guidance on why the patient is being referred, a delineated learning objective, and accompanying preparatory readings a “flipped visit.” We hypothesized a flipped visit would improve self-efficacy as well as interest and satisfaction. Methods Setting As part of their internal medicine core clerkship, Harvard Medical School students rotate through medicine subspecialty clinics. These students are in the second of study for their Doctor of Medicine (MD) degree, during the core clerkship year. so this rotation occurs during the transition from the classroom to patient care. Each year between 40 and 55 such students attend at least one hematology clinic at Brigham and Women’s Hospital or Dana Farber Cancer Institute as part of that experience. The “flipped visit” model We developed a structured “flipped visit” that included preassigned cases, delineated reason for referral, learning objects and preassigned readings. This last component is philosophically grounded in the flipped classroom [ 12 , 13 ] model and is intended to improve their knowledge of common hematologic conditions prior to arriving in clinic. A flipped visit is distinct from a flipped classroom, in that review of the patient’s chart is central and preparatory materials are read through the lens of how they apply to this individual case. We hypothesized that students could be empowered to conduct the visit more independently and focus more heavily on acquiring appropriate skills and attitudes, while consolidating their fund of knowledge. Several days before the assigned clinic, students received an email that assigned two specific patients, the reason for referral, accompanying focused reading materials related to the reason for the patients’ referrals, and learning objectives (see Fig. 1 ). They are asked to review the patients’ medical records and read the articles in advance, so that time in clinic could be focused on conducting the visit itself, as well as high yield and patient-specific teaching points. These readings were predominantly clinically focused review articles. On some occasions review articles were augmented by primary research articles published after the review articles if and only if these included key clinical implications that were not incorporated into reviews. While reviews were pulled from a breadth of journals and no single source made up a majority of the articles, the most common sources were the clinical applied “How I Treat” review series from Blood and clinical reviews from the New England Journal of Medicine [ 14 – 19 ]. Two hematology faculty members reviewed clinic schedules in order to make these assignments and find the appropriate readings. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Flipped visit model Surveys on self-efficacy and specialty interest All core clerkship students rotating through hematology clinic were eligible to participate in the study. The flipped visit was applied to all students attending clinic and not limited to students opting to participate, as we did not want to withhold what we thought would be a beneficial educational intervention. Students who did opt to participate were asked to complete pre- and post-clinic surveys. Survey responses were blinded, and core clerkship faculty were not aware in real time of the choice to participate. A $20 dollar gift was given after completion of the second survey, which required release of student names to the clerkship director, who was responsible for dispersing funds, after completion of the rotation. Gift cards were provided by the medicine clerkship as part of quality improvement efforts. There was no formal grant or funding. This study was deemed exempt by the Harvard Medical School Institutional Review Board (IRB) as it was considered a quality improvement project and was not assigned a trial number. Given that the study was deemed exempt by this IRB, written informed consent was not obtained. Students attending hematology clinic were contacted via email with the option to participate by responding to that email with their interest. If they expressed interest in participating, they were then sent survey links scheduled around their clinic sessions. The email was sent by a faculty member with a nonevaluative role. Surveys initially contained three domains: knowledge assessment, self-efficacy, and interest/satisfaction. The full surveys are available in the Supplemental Appendix. The five-question knowledge assessment was discarded early in the study, as the pathology of cases assigned in clinic was too variable for students to be considered exposed to one of the five pre-selected topics. Self-efficacy questions were derived from a validated self-efficacy questionnaire (see Table 1 ) [ 20 ]. The remainder of questions were newly generated. Interest/satisfaction questions covered interest in hematology and internal medicine as well as a subjective comparison to other clinical experiences. Domains for self-efficacy and interest/satisfaction were assessed on a five-point Likert scale. Distributions for each survey item were compared pre- and post-clinic and tested for statistical significance with Chi-square tests. Table 1. Survey items Self-Efficacy Items(20) How confident are you in your ability to: Use effective listening skills when interacting with a patient? Demonstrate caring when counseling a patient? Evaluate evidence from scientific studies relevant to your patients’ health problems? Practice cost-effective healthcare delivery that does not compromise quality of care? For hematology patients, how confident are you in your ability to: Develop an appropriate differential diagnosis? Generate a patient-specific treatment plan? Interest/Satisfaction Items I am interested in a career in hematology I am interested in a career in internal medicine Are you interested in additional hematology experiences? Do you think hematology is relevant to your future career? How satisfied were you with your clinic experience? * Relative to other medicine subspecialty clinics, how would you rate your experience in hematology clinic? * What components of hematology clinic provided a better experience compared to other medicine subspecialty clinics? * Relative to clinics in other non-medicine clerkships, how would you rate your experience in hematology clinic? * What components of hematology clinic provided a better experience compared to clinics in other non-medicine clerkships? * Open in a new tab *On post-clinic survey only Results Study participation During the two years from May 2021 through April 2023, 103 students rotated through hematology clinic. Of these 103 eligible students, 38 individuals (37%) opted to participate in the study. Two of the 38 students who completed the pre-clinic survey did not complete the post-clinic survey. Assessment of self-efficacy Amongst the six survey items on self-efficacy, students had a statistically significant increase in four of these measures (see Fig. 2 ). Self-efficacy increased significantly for evaluation of research relevant to patient care ( p = 0.008) and practicing cost-effective care ( p = 0.001). Self-efficacy specific to hematology also increased for creation of both a differential diagnosis ( p < 0.001) and a treatment plan ( p < 0.001). Two measures of self-efficacy were not changed: effective listening and demonstration of caring. These two measures had the highest pre-clinic self-efficacy at 28 of 38 (74%) and 25 of 38 (66%) reporting feeling very or extremely confident in their skills. Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Self-efficacy Measures Comparison to other clinics Students were asked to compare their experience in hematology clinic to other clinics including other medicine subspecialties and non-medicine specialties. In this comparison, the majority of students rated hematology clinic slightly or much better than other medicine subspecialty clinics (75%) and non-medicine clinics (71%). The most common reasons (see Fig. 3 ) students cited for the improved experience were confidence in discussing the case (57% vs. other medicine subspecialty, 54% vs. non-medicine), better understanding of the reason for referral (71% vs. other medicine subspecialty, 49% vs. non-medicine), and more knowledge acquired from the encounter (71% vs. other medicine subspecialty, 57% vs. non-medicine). Students did not separately rate their experience at these other clinics, but rather were asked comparative questions across the experiences. Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Experience relative to other clinics ( n = 35) Interest in hematology and internal medicine Four survey items addressed interest in hematology and internal medicine (See Fig. 4 ): interest in a career in Internal Medicine or Hematology, relevance of hematology to their future career and interest in additional hematology experiences. Many students reported interest in hematology and/or internal medicine even prior to attending clinic. However, while there were small shifts in distribution for these four survey items, none were statistically significant. Fig. 4. Open in a new tab Interest in hematology and internal medicine Knowledge assessments Prior to developments of the study protocol, students were primarily assigned cases for one of five diagnoses: anemia, venous thromboembolism, monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance, hereditary hemochromatosis, and myeloproliferative neoplasms. While conducting the study, it became rapidly apparent that shifts in practice patterns due to redistribution to malignant hematology divisions and utilization of eConsults for some diagnoses, changed the distribution of assigned cases. As such, prior to viewing results, the decision was made not to analyze these questions, as there would be too few students in each category to be able to draw inference. Discussion Our intervention transformed a passiveeducational experience into a highly valued one. The concern that passive experiences in clinic driven by the rapid pace and rigid structure of the ambulatory setting undermined self-efficacy and education prompted development of flipped visit approach to hematology clinic students in clinic. This consisted of assigning patients, reason for referral, learning objectives, and preparatory readings several days prior to clinic. Our results show that the flipped visit significantly improved self-efficacy on four distinct parameters as well as perceived clinical experience. This held across both individual skills and global reports of their experience. Our study found that students had improved self-efficacy in the application of data to provide cost effective care and research relevant to patient care. They were also more confident in their ability to make hematology-specific differential diagnoses and treatment plans. Achieving detectable change on these measures with a single clinic experience reflects significant educational growth, which is concordant with students’ reports that they gained more from these visits than in other clinics. Students’ self-efficacy did not change for effective listening and demonstration of caring, which were also skills that were highly self-rated prior to attending clinic. As such, it may be that students had already acquired sufficient self-efficacy from other clinical experience that precluded as much room for growth during hematology clinic. Alternatively, these may not have been the skills emphasized during the encounter. While not limited solely to the role of self-efficacy, student preference for hematology clinic compared to other medicine subspecialities and non-medicine specialties clinics indicates a more valued experience for them. The most frequently reported reasons for the improved experience align with self-efficacy theory including self-confidence discussing the case with an attending and more knowledge gained from the visit. While less directly linked to the intellectual grounding of our study, the other common response, a better understanding of the reason for referral, implies a student is more likely to appropriately reapply knowledge to other patients. This report of self-perceived mastery of material also demonstrates the intimate link between self-efficacy theory [ 3 , 5 ] and internal motivation levers, which focus on the domains of autonomy, mastery and purpose as drivers of motivation [ 21 ]. Students prepared to practice more independently reported more mastery, and, therefore, are expected to have accelerated acquisition of further mastery. In an era where medical education is shifting towards competency-based evaluation, both evaluators and students are likely to focus on mastery. Delineating what mastery entails can be complex at any stage of education [ 22 ]. The development of Entrustable Professional Activities (EPAs) as metrics of mastery for medical students has helped educators assess and communicate competency and have been adopted in many medical training systems [ 23 ]. However, measuring competency and motivating people along the way towards such mastery are distinct. Self-efficacy theory and the flipped visit align harmoniously with the underpinning of EPAs, while providing guidance on how to move students along the path from unentrustable to entrustable. Concrete methods such as the flipped visit may also help alleviate the tension between the benefits of EPAs and concern that teaching to EPAs can create checkbox behavior [ 24 ]. Similarly, self-directed learning (SDL) theory, which encompasses similar principles to self-efficacy theory, including taking a student-centric approach and emphasizing the importance of active learning, uses a frame that speaks to the need for life long learning as a practicing physician [ 25 ]. However, SDL requires students to know what they need to accomplish. The design of the flipped visit to include a delineated reason for referral and a learning objective for each patient allows students to both guide their own learning and measure their success. This impact on SDL may underlie some of the success of the invention. Increasing interest in a career in hematology was a secondary objective of our study, given the current and projected deficits of non-malignant hematologists [ 6 , 7 ]. There was no clear change in interest in hematology or internal medicine during the study. While students did find hematology relevant to their careers, this did not change significantly with exposure. Student assignment to hematology clinic was not random. While not all students assigned had requested hematology, students are permitted to request which specialty clinics they would be assigned to during their ambulatory month. As such, there was likely a selection bias into clinic towards students who already had a high baseline interest and exposure. This may explain why even an otherwise highly rated clinic experience did not increase expressed interest. Additionally, a single clinic may have been insufficient exposure to change career interest. Our study has several limitations. First, as alluded to, there was likely selection bias into hematology clinic attendance. There were four of 38 students who reported being either very or extremely interested in a career in hematology, which is unlikely to be representative of the general rate of interest of medical students. This may indicate a greater level of motivation and preparation than can be expected from the educational intervention alone. Additionally, just over a third (37%) of students who attended hematology clinic chose to participate in the study. As such, there may be compounded bias in the study sample. This may reflect those with a greater interest in internal medicine, hematology, or medical education, which could undermine the generalizability of our results to all medical students. We do not have available demographics for students who opted to participated or those who did not, so it is possible that our sample was non-representation on dimensions apart from career interest. Future research may benefit from understanding whether a flipped visit or the impact on self-efficacy more broadly has different outcomes based on demographic or educational background. We also cannot exclude the possibility that our results reflect a Hawthorne effect: students may have changed their behavior in clinic because they knew the study would ask after their learning experience. Though students took the pre- and post-clinic surveys within a few days of hematology clinic, we also cannot exclude the possibility that some of the growth measured was driven by other interceding activities. This may be quite credible for some measure of self-efficacy, but it would be a difficult attribution for hematology specific measure or implications of comparisons to other clinics. In comparing their experience to other clinics, it is possible that recall bias could have undermined results. Students were participating in other medicine subspecialty clinics at the same rotation as hematology clinic, while non-medicine clinics were more separated in time. It is also possible that the receipt of personalized emails with case assignments made the students feel more care for or invested in, which could have influenced responses. While this latter point may have impacted survey responses, this could also be viewed as an additional spillover benefit for student well-being and interest. While self-reporting could be considered an additional limitation, in the setting of self-efficacy theory, self-perception is central to growth and ought not to be characterized as a limitation of the study, but rather a limitation of the theory itself. Similarly, the survey questions were answered by Likert scale, which may have influenced answers. Free text answer options were provided but they were not utilized. Though the intervention was conducted in hematology clinic, there is no inherent reason why it would not be applicable to other specialty clinics or ambulatory care more broadly, nor why the impact would be specific to the US context. The same challenges and constraints are present in other clinics whenever students are first engaging in clinical care. Consultation visits across different specialties including other medicine subspecialties as well as many non-medicine specialties, also have a reason for referral that should be identifiable in advance with a corresponding fund of knowledge that can be augmented in advance with preparatory readings. As such, our findings are likely to reflect an effective strategy for improving education in many ambulatory settings and not solely hematology clinic, thought this merits direct study. Our study did not formally assess other potential spillover benefits from the flipped visit. Physicians accustomed to precepting students don’t need additional data to appreciate that it can be difficult for faculty to provide a good learning environment for students without either creating delays for patients or taking on a burden of deferred work themselves. Allowing students to conduct a greater proportion of a clinic visit independently and/or come prepared with a basic understanding of the relevant condition allows faculty time to be used for practice-based teaching. The hematology preceptors did report informally that this was their experience of the flipped visit. Given the increasing focus on physician burnout, systems based changes are needed in clinical education models to assure that high quality training of the next generation of physicians does not add to distress for currently practicing physicians [ 26 ]. This is especially true in fields already facing shortages, where students are more concentrated with the few available faculty and shortages may intensify clinic schedules. Sustainable educational interventions should permit students as well as preceptors to maximize the time allotted during clinic. The flipped visit may improve the experience for both students and preceptors, rather than asking additional effort by preceptors on behalf of students or expecting student to perform at a level of efficiency beyond that of more senior trainees. Additionally, the structure of the flipped visit can serve to provide guidance on best practices and expectations for preceptors. Formal education on teaching methods or educational theory is a rarity for practicing physicians and may render efforts inefficient or ineffective [ 27 , 28 ]. Efforts to develop metrics for teaching competency, such as teaching EPAs [ 29 ] acknowledge that preceptors need more tools to execute best practices and highlight that motivating students will remains a core skill. Student well-being was not the target of the flipped visit, nor was our intervention proposed to combat student burnout, but the levers of motivation and sense of self-efficacy appear as a potential countervailing force. Evaluations of burnout and well-being provide ample evidence that purposefulness and self-confidence are protective traits across many medical specialties [ 30 – 32 ]. Additionally, the individual attention intrinsic to the flipped visit (i.e. a personalized plan for a clinic session received via email) may help students feel valued and welcome. Future research should not only validate our findings for other specialties and other medical training systems, but also formally evaluate the impact on faculty. Fully delineating the benefits will help provide justification for expanding this approach, as, while generally saving faculty time, a hematology faculty member did have to expend non-clinic time assigning cases. In order to be adopted widely, educational advances should be sustainable, as well as beneficial to students. Conclusion A flipped visit approach to hematology clinic with pre-assigned cases, reason for referral, learning objectives, and accompanying readings significantly improved self-efficacy. Students also reported greater skill advancement from this approach compared to other clinics. This reflects an accessible approach to improving the education of medical students in ambulatory settings. Electronic supplementary material Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. Supplementary Material 1 (16.2KB, docx) Supplementary Material 2 (17.8KB, docx) Acknowledgements The authors would like to thank the faculty of the American Society of Hematology Medical Education Institute program for input into study design and recruitment. Author contributions ALL, ADP, NLK, JCK and NYO all designed the study, edited the manuscript and approved its submission. ALL drafted the manuscript and made the figures. Funding No grant was obtained. Gift certificates were supported through departmental quality improvement funds as noted. Data availability The datasets analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate This study was deemed exempted by the Harvard Medical School Institutional Review Board as it was considered to fall within quality improvement. As such, written informed consent was not required or collected. Consent for publication N/A. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Bloom BS. Taxonomy of Educational Objectives: The Classification of Educational Goals. 2. Flexner A. Medical education in the United States and Canada. From the Carnegie Foundation for the Advancement of Teaching, Bulletin Number Four, 1910. Bull World Health Organ. 2002;80(7):594–602. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Bandura A. Self-efficacy: toward a unifying theory of behavioral change. Psychol Rev. 1977;84(2):191–215. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Artino AR. Academic self-efficacy: from educational theory to instructional practice. Perspect Med Educ. 2012;1(2):76–85. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Bandura A, Freeman WH, Lightsey R. Self-Efficacy: the Exercise of Control. J