tz RD, Baskin WN, Frakes JT. Flexible fiberoptic sigmoidoscopy training for primary care physicians: results of a 5-year experience. Gastrointestinal Endo. 1989;35:316–20. doi: 10.1016/s0016-5107(89)72800-5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Hawes R, Lehman GA, Hast J, Ô''connor KW, Crabb DW, Lui A, et al. Training resident physicians in fiberoptic sigmoidoscopy. How many supervised examinations are required to achieve competence? Am J Med. 1986;80:465–70. doi: 10.1016/0002-9343(86)90721-7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Wigton RS. Measuring procedural skills. Ann Intern Med. 1996;125:1003–4. doi: 10.7326/0003-4819-125-12-199612150-00013. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Gross EB. Gender differences in physician stress. J Am Med Women''s Assoc. 1992;47:107–12. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Shapiro J, McGrath E, Anderson RC. Patients'', medical students'', and physicians'' perceptions of male and female physicians. Percept Motor Skills. 1983;56:179–90. doi: 10.2466/pms.1983.56.1.179. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',20,'2025-10-13 13:05:35','2025-10-13 13:05:35');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2351,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11707892','Factors influencing choice of residency program among medical intern doctors and medical students: a cross-sectional survey.','Al Ajmi A, Kashoub M, Al-Busaidi IS, Al Rashdi M, AlHussaini M, Alsaidi A, Al Rawahi M, Al Alawi AM.','2025','omani qaboos omani dispense lately intensifies oversupply oversupply qaboos squ squ deterrents allure qaboos omani omani gorenflo omani omani qaboos qaboos qaboos squ mrec conhs ebc0021 qaboos squ governorate muscat dakhilia dahira batinah batinah burimi sharqia sharqia dhufar musandam squh squh squh qaboos governorate sky governorate closeness governorate muscat dakhilia dahira batinah batinah burimi sharqia shargia dhufar musandam squh squh qaboos memorable inherit childless governorate muscat dakhilia dahira batinah batinah burimi sharqia shargia dhufar musandam squh hematopathology squh qaboos memorable memorable inherit keenly keenly undervalued serviced downfall laskin swayed expatriate omani qaboos squ sohar ajmi kashoub hussaini alsaidi rawahi busaidi alawai qaboos deanship mede qaboos squ mrec conhs ebc0021 meckel nyoni mulitalo cuadrado sumitani uncategorized longwoods uncategorized scheffler kinfu poz wpp sultanate dividend tradingeconomics trading tradingeconomics matri demogr abdulhadi maniri lawati qasmi feminization rouse l6219 s11739 levaillant levaillant lerolle vallet broza clyne reinert laban gowans brenneis vrr brandao barral netto e0133585 saigal takemura nishiue fetters morio otaki gorenflo ruffin craveiro dussault feminization hedden barer mcgrail bourgeault feminization rouse agren gebhard e016822 dotters pieper moms couper marais redress rrh mbemba gic hamelin brabant stfm familymedicine kost melaka manipal jstage jst bst 5_2011 _article pjms pjms alyazidi gaddoury alotibi aljehani alhudaifi zubaidi albuqaish marei nugud avicenna abdulghani alhujayri alohaideb alsaeed alshohayeb sup1 s25 winkel telzak hollond magro tandfonline asmepublications tb00825 laskin riska emhj emro emhj pmc6417223','Abstract Introduction The global shortage of healthcare professionals is a critical concern, with an estimated deficit of 18 million workers by 2030, particularly in low- and middle-income countries. Oman faces challenges with its doctor-to-population ratio falling below the global average. This study examines the factors influencing residency program choices among Omani medical students and interns, with a specific focus on internal medicine. Methods A cross-sectional survey using a validated, context-specific questionnaire was distributed to medical interns and clinical clerkship students at Sultan Qaboos University and the National University.Descriptive statistics and univariate analyses were used to explore demographic patterns and factors influencing specialty choices, including internal medicine. Results Out of 803 distributed surveys, 459 responses were collected, yielding a response rate of 58.8%. The study comprised 459 participants, with 26.1% males and 73.9% females, and an average age of 23.85 years (SD = 1.70). Internal medicine was selected by 19.9% of participants. Key factors influencing the choice of internal medicine included societal respect and a comfortable specialty department atmosphere. Gender differences showed that men prioritized factors such as aptitude for the specialty, attainable lifestyle, and risk of malpractice lawsuits, while women focused on the workplace environment. Role models played an important role in career decisions, with 25.7% of participants identifying a physician role model. Conclusion Internal medicine emerged as a popular specialty among Omani medical students and interns, driven by intellectual stimulation, job security, and work-life balance. Addressing gender-specific influences and expanding mentorship opportunities are essential to optimizing career guidance. The findings also emphasize the need for targeted strategies to address rural healthcare professional shortages. Clinical trial number Not applicable. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12909-024-06576-8. Keywords: Internal medicine, Residency choice, Medical students, Career decision, Gender differences, Oman Introduction The global deficit of competent medical professionals is an urgent problem influencing health systems all over the world, compromising nations’ abilities to dispense essential health services, respond to emerging health needs, and ensure overall health security [ 1 ]. The world is facing a rising shortage of healthcare workers, predicted to reach an 18 million deficit by the year 2030, which is a significant increase from the 7 million shortages registered in 2013. Furthermore, this shortage is predicted to rise to 12.9 million skilled health professionals by 2035 [ 1 , 2 ]. The lack of medical professionals is more substantial in low-to-middle-income countries and rural areas, with the latter reporting 56% of their population as being devoid of health coverage, more than twice the figure observed in urban communities [ 2 , 3 ]. Lately, Oman’s population has exceeded 5 million, with a notable upward trajectory in life expectancy reaching approximately 78.58 years for both genders in 2023 [ 4 ]. According to official national statistics, over 50% of the current population falls within the 25–64 age range [ 5 ]. Despite this, Oman is experiencing a significant decline in both fertility and mortality rates, attributed to diverse socioeconomic factors. This demographic shift indicates a forthcoming rise in the elderly population over the next 20–30 years, posing increased challenges to the healthcare system [ 6 ]. Oman faces a particularly acute deficit of qualified doctors, evident in its doctor-to-population ratio, which is lower than that of many other countries. As indicated by the World Bank collection of development indicators, the doctor-to-population ratio in Oman was approximately 1.7 physicians per 1,000 people in 2020, falling below the global average of 3.5 physicians per 1,000 people [ 7 ]. This shortage is especially pronounced in primary healthcare, where the demand for general practitioners (GPs) is rising to meet the population’s healthcare needs. The limited number of GPs, alongside the overall shortage of doctors, intensifies the pressure on Oman’s healthcare system, emphasizing the urgent necessity for more medical professionals, particularly in the primary care sector [ 7 ]. The Arab world has an average of 8.5 medical doctors per 10,000 population, though this varies widely across countries. For instance, Somalia has just 0.6 doctors per 10,000 population, while Qatar has 17.5. Arab countries can be grouped into categories based on medical staffing: some countries have low medical density and rely on foreign doctors (e.g., Yemen, Somalia), others have strengthened local medical training and may face oversupply risks (e.g., Algeria, Syria), while countries like Egypt and Jordan have relatively high medical density and export medical manpower. Oil-rich nations with high medical density, such as Saudi Arabia and the UAE, still rely heavily on foreign-trained physicians. This diversity reflects both shortages and oversupply of doctors across the region, underscoring the need for collaborative action in workforce planning [ 8 ].Several factors contribute to the shortage, including an increase in chronic diseases and limited investment in medical education and training [ 1 ]. The proportion of female medical students and doctors in Oman has increased significantly in recent years. For example, data from the Ministry of Health (MoH) and Sultan Qaboos University (SQU) show that female enrollment in medical schools and residency programs has risen steadily, with women now representing a substantial portion of the workforce. Specifically, 64% of medical students at SQU were female as of 2015, and women made up 42% of the total doctor workforce in the MoH, reflecting a broader national trend toward greater female participation in medicine [ 9 ]. Internal medicine is a cornerstone of the medical profession, offering comprehensive, patient-centered care that spans a wide range of conditions and diseases. Its holistic approach, integration with subspecialties, and critical role in both acute and primary care settings underline its enduring significance [ 10 ]. Additionally, specialists in this field manage comorbidities and provide holistic, person-centered care, especially beneficial for elderly patients with multiple long-term conditions [ 11 ]. The deficiency of qualified doctors in Oman includes the field of internal medicine (IM), a pivotal specialty within the healthcare system responsible for addressing the needs of chronically ill and medically complex patients. Globally, there has been a diminishing interest among medical students in enrolling in IM training programs, a trend warranting closer examination [ 12 ]. Over the years, there has been a shift in medical students’ perceptions of careers in IM, marked by increasingly negative attitudes toward managing complex medical issues over time [ 12 ]. A comprehensive survey of medical students conducted in 1990 revealed that factors such as the demanding time and workload of IM, perceived physician satisfaction in the field, and the types of patients encountered were significant deterrents to pursuing a career in internal medicine [ 13 ]. Many studies have been conducted across diverse regions globally to identify the factors influencing the choice of medical specialties among students [ 13 – 19 ]. These determinants include individual traits, features tied to medical curricula, perceived advantages, and the allure of specialties. Intern doctors are recent medical school graduates who participate in a supervised clinical training program, typically lasting one year, aimed at gaining practical experience and essential skills before progressing to residency programs. In Oman, there exists a single internship program under the College of Medicine and Health Sciences at Sultan Qaboos University Hospital. Annually, approximately 300 intern doctors enroll in this program, with the number varying based on the graduating medical students from both Oman and overseas institutions. A significant portion of these graduates typically applies for local residency programs administered by the Oman Medical Specialty Board. Over the past five years, the number of applicants to the internal medicine residency program has exhibited fluctuation, varying between 20 and 50 applicants annually. These fluctuations raise concerns as they may result in notable shortages of internal medicine physicians, particularly considering the anticipated aging population and the rise in chronic non-communicable diseases. Given the growing demand for healthcare professionals in Oman, it is essential to investigate the factors influencing the residency program choices of Omani medical students and intern doctors. This study aims to provide a comprehensive analysis of the decision-making process involved in selecting internal medicine and other specialties as future career paths. By examining the motivations and influences on these decisions, we seek to understand the key factors that shape the career trajectories of medical students and interns in Oman. Methods and materials Study design and selection criteria This study used a cross-sectional survey approach to evaluate the factors influencing medical students’ and intern doctors’ choice of medical specialization in Oman. The survey instrument used is a modified version of a Takeda et al. validated questionnaire that was modified to take into account local demands and settings [ 20 ]. The survey was anonymously administered to intern doctors and medical students in their clinical clerkship years, excluding non-Omani intern doctors whose postgraduate training was not locally supported. Sample size calculation and questionnaire adjustment Considering the annual output of medical school graduates in Oman, approximately ranging from 200 to 250, and our target audience, which includes intern doctors and medical students in their last 3 years of medical school, our estimated target population size was approximately 1000 individuals. In line with conventional survey research guidelines, it is recommended to have a sample size equivalent to at least 5–10% of the population size. Consequently, our survey would require a sample size ranging from 50 to 100 subjects. For our survey, we employed a previously validated questionnaire developed by Takeda and colleagues in 2013, which is grounded in the theory of reasoned action (TRA) [ 20 ]. This theory posits that an individual’s behavior is influenced by their intention to perform the behavior, shaped by two key components: attitude towards the behavior (determined by beliefs about outcomes and their importance) and subjective norms (involving beliefs about how close individuals perceive the behavior and the motivation to meet their expectations) [ 17 ]. The variables used in the questionnaire were adapted from a study by Gorenflo et al., presenting a “model of medical student specialty choice based on the theory of reasoned action“ [ 21 ]. The questionnaire comprises 45 questions in total, including 14 general inquiries about participant demographics and 31 specific queries aimed at evaluating factors influencing specialty selection. During the survey development, four questions from the original survey by Takeda et al. were excluded due to their lack of relevance to the Oman medical education system or geographical context. Additionally, two questions were adjusted to align with the characteristics of our local medical education system (supplementary 1). The questionnaire validation process was guided by a structured approach to identify the primary themes and domains relevant to specialty choice, including personal interest, perceived job market demand, societal factors, and lifestyle considerations. These domains were defined based on a review of the relevant literature and adapted to suit the cultural and educational context of Oman. Each theme was reviewed and refined by expert members of the research team to ensure that all pertinent factors influencing specialty choice were included. To ensure the validity of the questionnaire, we sought input from faculty members and medical education experts, who reviewed each item for clarity, relevance, and cultural appropriateness, ensuring the content was both comprehensive and contextually appropriate for the Omani setting. To assess the reliability of the instrument, a pilot test was conducted with a small group of medical students and interns. This pilot test helped evaluate the clarity, comprehension, and consistency of the questions. Based on their feedback, we made minor adjustments to improve item clarity and ensure the questionnaire accurately reflected the factors influencing specialty choice among Omani medical students and interns. The feedback also helped ensure that the questionnaire was consistent in measuring the intended constructs, contributing to its overall reliability. Participants and questionnaire administration The survey was distributed across the only two medical schools in Oman: Sultan Qaboos University and the National University. A total of 803 surveys were distributed: 125 to 6th -year students and 127 to 7th -year students at the National University, 125 to 6th -year students and 126 to 7th -year students at Sultan Qaboos University, and 300 surveys to medical interns who are graduates of both universities. The distribution and collection of the questionnaire were managed by our research team or administrators from November to December 2023. Participation in the survey was voluntary, and individual responses were kept anonymous. Ethics This study was approved by the Medical Research Ethics Committee of the College of Medicine and Health Sciences of Sultan Qaboos University (REF. NO. SQU-EC/ 211/2023 MREC #3115) as well as Ethics and Biosafety Committee (EBC) of the College of Medicine and Health Sciences of National University (REF. NO/CONHS/EBC0021/2023). Consent to publish the survey results was obtained from all participants. Data analysis Descriptive statistics were applied to present participant demographics, career options, and factors guiding these decisions. Age and other continuous variables were conveyed as means and standard deviations, whereas categorical variables like gender and marital status were represented as frequencies and percentages. For inferential analysis, the key outcome variable, career choice (internal medicine versus non-internal medicine), was dichotomized. Univariable logistic regression was utilized to examine the relationship between each explanatory variable (for example, gender, marital status, influence of role models) and career choice. A p-value of 0.05 or lower was deemed statistically significant, with a two-tailed significance threshold set at a p-value of less than 0.05. All statistical examinations were performed using STATA version 17.0 (STATA Corporation, College Station, TX, USA). Results The survey achieved 459 responses out of 803 potential participants, resulting in an overall response rate of 58.8%. The corresponding rates by group were: 72.0% (90 out of 125) for 6th -year students at the National University, 75.6% (96 out of 127) for its 7th -year students, 68.0% (85 out of 125) for 6th -year students at Sultan Qaboos University (SQU), 53.9% (68 out of 126) for its 7th -year students, and 40.0% (120 out of 300) for medical interns. The study included 459 participants, consisting of 120 males (26.1%) and 339 females (73.9%). Participants had an average age of 23.85 years, with a standard deviation of 1.70. Regarding marital status, 26 participants (5.7%) were married, and 433 (94.3%) were single. Only 6 participants (1.3%) had children, while 453 (98.7%) did not. The distribution of participants by governorate showed that 109 (23.8%) were from Muscat, 90 (19.7%) from Al Dakhilia, 31 (6.8%) from Al Dahira, 111 (24.2%) from North Al Batinah, 40 (8.7%) from South Al Batinah, 6 (1.3%) from Al Burimi, 41 (8.9%) from North Al Sharqia, 22 (4.8%) from South Al Sharqia, 6 (1.3%) from Dhufar, and 2 (0.4%) from Musandam. Most participants resided in urban areas (310, 67.6%), while 148 (32.4%) were from rural areas. Regarding their medical school, 20 (4.4%) studied overseas, 206 (44.9%) at SQUH, and 233 (50.7%) at NU. The educational level of participants included 114 interns (24.9%), 259 senior clerkship students (56.5%), and 85 junior clerkship students (18.5%). A small portion of participants had physician parents (38, 8.3%) or knew a close physician (82, 17.9%). Additionally, 118 participant s (25.7%) had a role model physician. The career choices varied, with family medicine being the choice for 75 participants (16.4%), internal medicine for 91 (19.9%), general surgery for 65 (14.2%), pediatrics for 44 (9.6%), and various other specialties each representing smaller percentages of the cohort (Table 1 ; Fig. 1 ). Notably, one student did not choice any specialty. Table 1. Overall respondents demographics and career plans Question Category Frequency ( n = 459) Age (Mean SD) 23.85 (1.70) Marital Status Married 26 (5.7%) Single 433 (94.3%) Have Children Yes 6 (1.3%) No 453 (98.7%) Residence Urban 310 (67.6%) Rural 148 (32.4%) Medical School Overseas 20 (4.4%) SQUH 206 (44.9%) NU 233 (50.7%) Educational Level Intern 114 (24.9%) Senior 259 (56.5%) Junior 85 (18.5%) Parents Physicians Yes 38 (8.3%) No 421 (91.7%) Knew a Close Physician Yes 82 (17.9%) No 377 (82.1%) Role Model Physician Yes 118 (25.7%) No 341 (74.3%) Type of Physician Highly specialized 110 (24.1%) Work with patients 145 (31.7%) Either 202 (44.2%) Open in a new tab Table 1 : SQUH: Sultan Qaboos University Hospit