e specialty department ( p = 0.038), both of which emerged as key influences in their decision-making process. Despite these significant differences, many other factors did not show statistical significance between the two groups. Interest in clinical work ( p = 0.216), specific organ-based practice ( p = 0.266), and working with targeted populations ( p = 0.326) were similar across participants regardless of their chosen specialty. Likewise, interest in research ( p = 0.431) and the prospect for further development ( p = 0.144) were comparable between internal medicine and non-internal medicine groups (refer Table 2 -B). Table 3. Career preferences and influences by specialty choice Question Category Non-Internal Medicine ( n = 367) Internal Medicine ( n = 91) Total ( n = 458) • p -value Type of Physician Highly specialized 91 (24.9%) 19 (20.9%) 110 (24.1%) 0.392 Work with patients 119 (32.5%) 26 (28.6%) 145 (31.6%) Either 156 (42.6%) 46 (50.6%) 202 (44.4%) Interest in Clinical Work Not at all 2 (0.54%) 1 (1.10%) 3 (0.65%) 0.216 Not particularly 15 (4.08%) 0 (0.00%) 15 (3.27%) Fairly well 116 (31.52%) 27 (29.67%) 143 (31.15%) Extremely 235 (63.86%) 63 (69.23%) 298 (64.92%) Interest in Organ Not at all 15 (4.08%) 3 (3.33%) 18 (3.93%) 0.266 Not particularly 61 (16.58%) 13 (14.44%) 74 (16.16%) Fairly well 146 (39.67%) 28 (31.11%) 174 (37.99%) Extremely 146 (39.67%) 46 (51.11%) 192 (41.92%) Interest in Targeted Population Not at all 41 (11.17%) 9 (10.00%) 50 (10.94%) 0.326 Not particularly 102 (27.79%) 34 (37.78%) 136 (29.76%) Fairly well 120 (32.70%) 25 (27.78%) 145 (31.73%) Extremely 104 (28.34%) 22 (24.44%) 126 (27.57%) Interest in Research Not at all 43 (11.68%) 7 (7.69%) 50 (10.89%) 0.431 Not particularly 112 (30.43%) 23 (25.27%) 135 (29.41%) Fairly well 123 (33.42%) 35 (38.46%) 158 (34.42%) Extremely 90 (24.46%) 26 (28.57%) 116 (25.27%) Interest in Surgical Procedures Not at all 49 (13.35%) 15 (16.48%) 64 (13.97%) 0.008* Not particularly 71 (19.35%) 19 (20.88%) 9