oportion of participants within each response category for internal medicine and non-internal medicine groups. Interest in clinical work, research, surgical procedures, and other factors was measured on a scale from ‘Not at all’ to ‘Extremely.’ ‘Highly specialized’ refers to physicians focusing on a specific medical subspecialty. p-value refers to the probability value used to determine the statistical significance of the observed differences between groups, A p-value of less than 0.05 (*) is considered statistically significant. (•) One student did not select any specialty Checking gender disparities, most factors related to career preferences and influences showed no significant differences between male and female participants. Marital status ( p = 0.409) and having children ( p = 0.687) were similar across genders, with the majority being single and childless. Residence type also showed no gender disparity ( p = 0.614), with both males and females predominantly residing in urban areas. In terms of familial and professional influences, the proportions of participants with physician parents ( p = 0.980), knowledge of a close physician ( p = 0.876), and identifying a role model physician ( p = 0.238) were consistent between the genders. Similarly, preferences for the type of physician role—whether highly specialized, working directly with patients, or flexible to either—did not differ significantly ( p = 0.212). Career choices showed no notable gender differences ( p = 0.143). Internal medicine was the most frequently selected specialty for both males and females, followed by family medicine and general surgery. Other specialties, such as pediatrics, dermatology, and emergency medicine, were chosen in comparable proportions by both genders. Overall, the majority of career preferences, familial influences, and lifestyle factors did not vary significantly between male and female participants (refer Table 3 -A). Table 4. Gender differences in career choices and influen