he availability of residency or training programs in Oman ( p = 0.013). In contrast, no significant gender differences were observed in interest in clinical work ( p = 0.782), research ( p = 0.595), working hours ( p = 0.060), or job availability ( p = 0.676). Responses regarding personal experiences, such as family or friends suffering from illness ( p = 0.236) or memorable experiences during medical school ( p = 0.389), were also consistent across genders. Regarding willingness to work in rural areas, no significant gender difference was observed ( p = 0.788). Both men and women showed similar proportions of keen motivation, willingness to work for a certain period, or preference to avoid rural practice (refer Table 3 -B). Table 5. Gender differences in responses to career choice influences Category Question /Options Male n = 120 (%) Female n = 339 (%) Total n = 459 (%) p -value Characteristics of the specialty Interest in clinical work 0.782 Not at all 0 (0.00%) 3 (0.88%) 3 (0.65%) Not particularly 4 (3.33%) 11 (3.24%) 15 (3.27%) Fairly well 38 (31.67%) 105 (30.97%) 143 (31.15%) Extremely 78 (65.00%) 220 (64.90%) 298 (64.92%) Interest in specific organ 0.115 Not at all 4 (3.36%) 14 (4.13%) 18 (3.93%) Not particularly 13 (10.92%) 61 (17.99%) 74 (16.16%) Fairly well 42 (35.29%) 132 (38.94%) 174 (37.99%) Extremely 60 (50.42%) 132 (38.94%) 192 (41.92%) Interest in targeted population 0.084 Not at all 13 (11.02%) 37 (10.91%) 50 (10.94%) Not particularly 25 (21.19%) 111 (32.74%) 136 (29.76%) Fairly well 46 (38.98%) 99 (29.20%) 145 (31.73%) Extremely 34 (28.81%) 92 (27.14%) 126 (27.57%) Interest in research 0.595 Not at all 12 (10.00%) 38 (11.21%) 50 (10.89%) Not particularly 31 (25.83%) 104 (30.68%) 135 (29.41%) Fairly well 47 (39.17%) 111 (32.74%) 158 (34.42%) Extremely 30 (25.00%) 86 (25.37%) 116 (25.27%) Interest in surgical procedures 0.003 Not at all 12 (10.08%) 52 (15.34%) 64 (13.97%) Not particularly 18 (15.13%) 72 (21.24%) 90 (19.65%) Fairly well 30 (25.21%) 11